          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Dilute sodium hydroxide, NaOH(aq), is electrolysed."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Identify the gas produced at the anode, and write a half-equation for the reaction involved."
          },
          {
            "type": "answerLines",
            "labels": [
              "gas produced at the anode",
              "half-equation"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "24.1(1)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 2,
          "instruction": null,
          "guidance": [
            "<b>I</b> ‘O’ alone.",
            "<b>I</b> state symbols.",
            "<b>A</b> multiples, use of ‘⇌’.",
            "<b>A</b> 4OH<sup>–</sup> – 4e<sup>–</sup> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + O<sub>2</sub>."
          ],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "<b>M1</b> Oxygen / O<sub>2</sub>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "<b>M2</b> 4OH<sup>–</sup> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + O<sub>2</sub> + 4e<sup>–</sup>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ],
          "source": {
            "ms": 7
          }
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "In dilute aqueous NaOH the ions present are Na<sup>+</sup>, OH<sup>–</sup> and (from water) H<sup>+</sup>. The anode attracts the anions, and OH<sup>–</sup> is the only anion, so it is discharged: it loses electrons and oxygen gas is given off. Oxidation happens at the anode, so the electrons appear on the right-hand side of the half-equation: 4OH<sup>–</sup> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + O<sub>2</sub> + 4e<sup>–</sup>. (Hydrogen is produced at the cathode, because H<sup>+</sup> is discharged in preference to Na<sup>+</sup>.)",
              "refs": [
                "ms:a#1",
                "ms:a#2",
                "syllabus:24.1(1)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "List the anions present: only OH⁻ (Na⁺ goes to the cathode).",
            "OH⁻ is oxidised at the anode, giving O₂.",
            "Balance O and H with H₂O, then balance charge with 4e⁻ on the right."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Writing ‘O’ instead of oxygen or O₂, which the MS ignores.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:a#1"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Naming hydrogen, which is the cathode product, as the anode gas.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:a#1"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Putting the electrons on the left-hand side, which turns the half-equation into a reduction.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:a#2"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
